@{
    Layout = "~/Areas/GitHub/Views/Shared/GithubLayout.cshtml";
    ViewData["Title"] = "Rebasing";
}

<main id="tutorials" class="container py-1">
    <div class="row g-4">

        <!-- Sidebar Navigation -->
        <aside class="col-lg-3">
            @Html.Partial("~/Areas/GitHub/Views/Shared/GithubTopics.cshtml")
        </aside>

        <!-- Reading Content Area -->
        <section class="col-lg-9">
            <div class="card border-0 shadow-sm">
                <div class="card-body p-4 p-lg-5">

                    <h1 class="fw-bold mb-3">Git Rebasing</h1>

                    <p>
                        In Git, <strong>rebasing</strong> is the process of
                        moving or replaying a sequence of commits onto a
                        new base commit. It is commonly used as an
                        alternative to merging when you want to maintain
                        a linear project history.
                    </p>

                    <p>
                        For example, if you created a feature branch from
                        <code>main</code> and new commits were added to
                        <code>main</code>, you can rebase your feature
                        branch to replay your commits on top of the latest
                        version of <code>main</code>.
                    </p>

                    <div class="alert alert-info">
                        <strong>Remember:</strong>
                        Rebasing rewrites commit history by creating new
                        commits with new hashes. Use it carefully when
                        working with shared branches.
                    </div>

                    <hr class="my-4" />

                    <!-- Rebase Workflow -->
                    <h3 class="fw-bold mb-3">1. How Rebasing Works</h3>

                    <p>Imagine the repository has this commit history:</p>

                    <pre class="bg-dark text-light p-3 rounded"><code>main:           A---B---C
                     \
feature:              D---E</code></pre>

                    <p>
                        The <code>main</code> branch receives another
                        commit, <code>F</code>. Rebasing the feature
                        branch onto <code>main</code> replays commits
                        <code>D</code> and <code>E</code> on top of
                        <code>F</code>.
                    </p>

                    <pre class="bg-dark text-light p-3 rounded"><code>main:           A---B---C---F
                                 \
feature:                         D'---E'</code></pre>

                    <p>
                        The commits <code>D'</code> and <code>E'</code>
                        contain the replayed changes and have new commit
                        identities.
                    </p>

                    <hr class="my-4" />

                    <!-- Common Commands -->
                    <h3 class="fw-bold mb-3">2. Common Rebase Commands</h3>

                    <h5 class="fw-semibold mt-4">Standard Rebase</h5>

                    <p>
                        Switch to your feature branch and rebase it onto
                        the latest local <code>main</code> branch.
                    </p>

                    <pre class="bg-dark text-light p-3 rounded"><code>git switch feature/login
git fetch origin
git rebase origin/main</code></pre>

                    <p>
                        <code>git fetch origin</code> downloads remote
                        updates. The rebase then replays your feature
                        commits on top of <code>origin/main</code>.
                    </p>

                    <h5 class="fw-semibold mt-4">Rebase During a Pull</h5>

                    <pre class="bg-dark text-light p-3 rounded"><code>git pull --rebase origin main</code></pre>

                    <p>
                        Fetches updates from the remote
                        <code>main</code> branch and replays your local
                        commits on top of the fetched history.
                    </p>

                    <p>
                        This command is typically used when you are on
                        a local branch that needs to incorporate changes
                        from the specified remote branch.
                    </p>

                    <h5 class="fw-semibold mt-4">Interactive Rebase</h5>

                    <pre class="bg-dark text-light p-3 rounded"><code>git rebase -i HEAD~3</code></pre>

                    <p>
                        Opens an interactive editor for the last three
                        commits. You can choose to reorder, squash,
                        reword, or drop commits.
                    </p>

                    <ul>
                        <li><strong>pick:</strong> Keep a commit.</li>
                        <li><strong>reword:</strong> Change a commit message.</li>
                        <li><strong>squash:</strong> Combine a commit with the previous commit.</li>
                        <li><strong>drop:</strong> Remove a commit from the rebased history.</li>
                    </ul>

                    <div class="alert alert-warning">
                        <strong>Important:</strong>
                        Interactive rebase changes commit history.
                        Avoid rewriting commits that others are already
                        using unless the team has agreed on the procedure.
                    </div>

                    <hr class="my-4" />

                    <!-- Rebase Conflicts -->
                    <h3 class="fw-bold mb-3">3. Resolving Rebase Conflicts</h3>

                    <p>
                        A rebase may stop when Git cannot automatically
                        apply a commit. Resolve the conflicts before
                        continuing.
                    </p>

                    <h5 class="fw-semibold mt-4">Step 1: Identify Conflicts</h5>

                    <pre class="bg-dark text-light p-3 rounded"><code>git status</code></pre>

                    <h5 class="fw-semibold mt-4">Step 2: Resolve the Files</h5>

                    <p>
                        Open each conflicted file, review the competing
                        changes, remove the conflict markers, and save
                        the correct code.
                    </p>

                    <h5 class="fw-semibold mt-4">Step 3: Stage the Resolved File</h5>

                    <pre class="bg-dark text-light p-3 rounded"><code>git add filename.ext</code></pre>

                    <h5 class="fw-semibold mt-4">Step 4: Continue Rebasing</h5>

                    <pre class="bg-dark text-light p-3 rounded"><code>git rebase --continue</code></pre>

                    <p>
                        Repeat these steps if another commit causes a
                        conflict during the rebase.
                    </p>

                    <h5 class="fw-semibold mt-4">Cancel the Rebase</h5>

                    <pre class="bg-dark text-light p-3 rounded"><code>git rebase --abort</code></pre>

                    <p>
                        Use this command to abandon the current rebase
                        and return the branch to its pre-rebase state,
                        provided the rebase is still in progress.
                    </p>

                    <hr class="my-4" />

                    <!-- Merge vs Rebase -->
                    <h3 class="fw-bold mb-3">4. Merge vs. Rebase</h3>

                    <div class="table-responsive">
                        <table class="table table-bordered table-striped align-middle">
                            <thead class="table-dark">
                                <tr>
                                    <th>Feature</th>
                                    <th>Merge</th>
                                    <th>Rebase</th>
                                </tr>
                            </thead>
                            <tbody>
                                <tr>
                                    <td>Purpose</td>
                                    <td>Combines branch histories.</td>
                                    <td>Replays commits onto another base.</td>
                                </tr>
                                <tr>
                                    <td>History</td>
                                    <td>Preserves existing commits; may create a merge commit.</td>
                                    <td>Creates new commits for the replayed changes.</td>
                                </tr>
                                <tr>
                                    <td>Conflict handling</td>
                                    <td>Resolve conflicts, then complete the merge.</td>
                                    <td>Resolve conflicts and continue the rebase.</td>
                                </tr>
                                <tr>
                                    <td>Common use</td>
                                    <td>Integrating branches while preserving branch history.</td>
                                    <td>Updating a feature branch with a linear history.</td>
                                </tr>
                            </tbody>
                        </table>
                    </div>

                    <hr class="my-4" />

                    <!-- Best Practices -->
                    <h3 class="fw-bold mb-3">5. Rebasing Best Practices</h3>

                    <ul>
                        <li>Commit or stash unfinished work before rebasing.</li>
                        <li>Use <code>git status</code> to check your repository state.</li>
                        <li>Rebase your own feature branches when appropriate.</li>
                        <li>Avoid rewriting shared commits without team coordination.</li>
                        <li>Test your code after the rebase completes.</li>
                    </ul>

                    <div class="alert alert-success mt-4">
                        <strong>Key Takeaway:</strong>
                        Use <code>git rebase</code> to replay commits onto
                        a new base and maintain a linear history. Use
                        <code>git rebase -i</code> to organize local commits,
                        <code>git rebase --continue</code> after resolving
                        conflicts, and <code>git rebase --abort</code>
                        to cancel an in-progress rebase.
                    </div>

                    <!-- Previous / Next Navigation -->
                    <div class="d-flex justify-content-between align-items-center gap-3 flex-wrap mt-5 pt-4 border-top">

                        <a class="btn btn-outline-primary"
                           href="@Url.Action("UndoingMistakes", "AdvancedGitAndHistory", new { area = "GitHub" })">
                            &larr; Previous: Undoing Mistakes
                        </a>

                        <a class="btn btn-primary"
                           href="@Url.Action("BlamingAuditing", "AdvancedGitAndHistory", new { area = "GitHub" })">
                            Next: Git Blame &amp; Auditing &rarr;
                        </a>

                    </div>

                </div>
            </div>
        </section>
    </div>
</main>
